From 1bd1d544948be7456e0696c0d85f32b017e1df60 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lordofthepigs <nicolas.piguet@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 21:49:33 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Add effect-record instrumentation hooks
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Eight hooks an external tool reads to record what each ability does when it
resolves. Every one is inert unless something installs a listener or reads the
pointer, and nothing in Forge does — the reader is a separate project that
builds against stock Forge and reaches these reflectively.

TriggerHandler gains a per-thread pointer to the trigger currently running.
Without it an event that arrives while a trigger resolves is attributed to
whatever else was on the stack, which is wrong exactly when two things resolve
at once. It also gains a listener at the condition evaluation, called with both
answers: a trigger that did not fire is what distinguishes the condition from
the base rate, and it is notified below the mode check so the negatives are
same-event-type candidates rather than every trigger in the game.

ReplacementHandler gains a listener around executeReplacement, the one point
every replacement passes through, called with the parameter map before and
after. The map is deep-copied before the call because replacements mutate it in
place; a shallow copy would show the outgoing values on both sides.

AbilityUtils gains a per-thread pointer to the sub-ability currently resolving,
so an outcome can be attributed to the clause that produced it rather than to
the whole line. Restored rather than cleared on the way out, since a
sub-ability can resolve another.

AiController gains two listeners. One is called with the rules-level verdict
for each candidate the AI evaluated — can-play, affordable, has-legal-target.
The other is called with the legal attacker and blocker sets, from
AiAttackController.refreshCombatants and AiBlockController.getPossibleBlockers,
both of which compute them and discard them once the AI has chosen. Neither
carries the AI's policy judgment: that is its opinion rather than the game's
rules.

StaticAbilityCantAttackBlock.cantAttack and cantBlockBy each already found the
static ability responsible and then reduced it to a boolean. The loop moves
into cantAttackStatic and cantBlockByStatic, which return that ability, and the
boolean methods delegate. Same answers, and a caller can now say which static
stopped a creature rather than only that one did.

AbilityManaPart gains a listener at the point produced mana reaches the pool.
Mana abilities never use the stack, so an observer watching casts and
resolutions sees no mana at all; the notification carries the mana after any
replacement has rewritten it.

Card gains five accessors, answering two halves of one question about a
continuous effect: what one static contributes, and what the card would be
without it.

getChangedCardTypesByStatic and getChangedCardColorsByStatic regroup the type
and colour layer tables under the static that wrote each entry, matching what
the keyword and P/T layers already expose. The existing accessors flatten them
into the applied result, which is what the rules need and which loses the
applying static's id. Each channel is spread over three tables — by-text,
character-defining, and the main layer — and the regrouping happens here, where
the layers are known, rather than in a caller that would take one of the three
and under-report without failing. The key is the static's id alone rather than
the (timestamp, id) pair the tables use, since one static can write to more
than one of these layers and the pairs would collide.

getTypeWithout, getColorWithout and getKeywordsWithout recombine the layers the
way getType, getColor and updateKeywordsCache do, with one static's entries
dropped, and return the answer instead of caching it. None of them touches the
card. Subtracting a static's contributions from the applied result is a
different and wrong answer: a second static granting the same keyword or type
would go with it, a card printed with what its own static grants would lose it,
and a static that overwrites a type line or strips a whole class of type cannot
be inverted from its contribution at all.

CardColor becomes public for the same reader. It is a two-component record of
what its accessor returns, and a caller outside this package cannot invoke a
record component whose declaring class it may not access.

GameAction.destroy puts the causing ability in the Destroyed trigger's run
parameters. It is in scope and was being dropped, so nothing downstream could
name what destroyed the permanent.
